\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let a finite group $G\leq\operatorname{Sym}(\Omega)$ act transitively on a finite set $\Omega$. For $g\in G$, let $\operatorname{Fix}_\Omega(g)=\{\omega\in\Omega:g\omega=\omega\}$. A derangement has no fixed points, but the requested conclusion also permits elements with at least two fixed points.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every such action and every distinct $\alpha,\beta\in\Omega$, is there a $g\in G$ such that
\[
 g\alpha=\beta\qquad\text{and}\qquad|\operatorname{Fix}_\Omega(g)|\ne1?
\]
No primitivity assumption or requirement that $g$ be a derangement is imposed. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can every ordered pair of distinct points in a finite transitive action be joined by an element having either zero or at least two fixed points?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2608 (KOU-21.99). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] P. Müller, Problem 21.99, in E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), \emph{The Kourovka Notebook: Unsolved Problems in Group Theory}, 21st edition, arXiv:1401.0300v48 (6 October 2026). This version records a refutation; the displayed question is the historically registered target. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}.
\end{description}
